# The merge-depth lever: descriptors saved by packing planes adjacent, priced against the floor
# Host DESKTOP-CBL5NUA (Desktop 2, Ryzen 7 8700G), 2026-09-20, env mlir-aie-iron
# OFFLINE. No container was built, no hardware context was opened, xrt-smi was not consulted.
#
# Command:  python tools/merge_depth_sizing.py
#
# MEASURED   the control row of each model: 2,972 (yolov8n) and 7,143 (yolov8s) DMA tasks per
#            frame, asserted by the tool against results/aie/split_segment_floor_phoenix_20260920.log.
#            The floor split and us/task come from results/aie/object_size_repricing_20260920.log.
# DERIVED    every other row.
#
## Why descriptors, and not bytes
#
# The floor is 69.6%-75.4% of every dispatch in this family and 77%-81% of the floor is not
# transfer, so per-task issue is the dominant cost in this engine. Task count is set by how
# deeply fills merge, and merge depth is set by ONE rule:
#
#   canonical() folds contiguous dimensions. merge_quad folds a quad's four fills into one task
#   and adds a dimension. merge_runs folds up to 64 consecutive same-shape fills into one task
#   and adds a dimension. BOTH refuse a pattern that already has four dimensions.
#
# So a fill must be at most TWO-dimensional after canonical() to receive both merges.
#
#   k3s2 emits (16, 400)      2-D  -> quad -> runs   folds 23.6 (n) / 39.6 (s) packets per task
#   k1   emits (8, 5, 160)    3-D  -> quad -> XX     folds 4.90 (n) / 4.71 (s)
#   k3s1 emits (4, 8, 200)    3-D  -> quad -> XX     folds 4.42 (n) / 4.09 (s)
#
# k1's rows fold into its bytes only when the pitch is 160 B, i.e. a 20-pixel-wide map, so on
# every wider map it stays three-dimensional. k1 is the largest single kind: 38% of yolov8s's
# activation fills, stuck at a quarter of the merge depth k3s2 gets for free.
#
## What was simulated
#
# Each selected kind's fill is replaced by a CONTIGUOUS run of A_BYTES at the same offset, and
# the real scheduler re-merges it. That substitution is exactly what tools/fill_layout_sizing.py
# calls "planes packed adjacent": set the plane stride to five times the pitch and k1's
# (8, 5, 160) at strides (plane_bytes, pitch, 1) becomes (40, 160), which is two-dimensional.
# Activation BYTE counts are identical in every scenario below - 83,072,000 B (yolov8n) and
# 237,670,400 B (yolov8s) - so the task saving is on unchanged traffic.
#
## What it costs
#
# Packing planes adjacent is byte-free only where a kind's chained windows abut.
# tools/fill_layout_sizing.py measured that per shape: k1 abuts (step 5 rows >= 5 rows per
# packet, 1.00x bytes) while k3s1 replicates 8 of 5 stepped rows (1.60x), k1up2 2.00x and pool
# 3.20x. Those factors are charged below as transport at the measured 6.898931 GB/s per column.
#
