## Reading it

k1 alone is BYTE-FREE and worth -0.393 ms (yolov8n) and -2.163 ms (yolov8s). On yolov8s that
is 7.5x the 0.29 ms gap to AMD's stack that was accepted as a known runtime limitation on
2026-09-16, and it would move a 17.894 ms dispatch to about 15.7 ms.

k3s1 costs 1.60x on its own bytes and is worth MORE THAN k1 on top of it: -2.44 ms of task
time against +0.278 ms of transport on yolov8s, net a further -2.17 ms. Taking both gives
-4.328 ms, which would put the dispatch near 13.6 ms.

That reverses an earlier judgement, and the reason is worth recording. The 2026-09-20 layout
sizing measured the same replication factors and declined every kind but k1 on them -
"would replicate overlapping rows (1.0-3.2x bytes): 929 = 50%, not proposed". That decision
priced bytes against a model that charged core-side traffic at the DDR rate and over-counted
transport 3.10x (results/aie/object_size_repricing_20260920.log). Re-priced against the
measured floor, where transport is 20% and per-task issue 80%, the declined half is the more
valuable one.

The same sizing estimated the byte-free half at 940 descriptors collapsing to 59 on yolov8n,
i.e. -881 tasks. This run measures -276 by putting the real offsets through the real
merge_runs, which enforces constant spacing, identical shape and the 64-repeat ceiling. The
earlier figure assumed every candidate folds at the ceiling; it is 3.2x optimistic, and -276
is the number to plan against.

## Not established

  - No container was built and nothing was dispatched. The control is the only row with
    silicon behind it, and it is behind it only as a task count.
  - The offsets are today's. A real repack moves them, so this bounds merging under today's
    placement rather than predicting a built layout.
  - Workspace capacity is not modelled. Interleaving channel blocks at a five-row band changes
    how plan_workspace sizes and reuses slots, and that could cost memory or reuse.
  - Neither is the lock and barrier structure a repack disturbs, which is what made the
    activation ring fail twice.
  - The replication factors were measured on yolov8n's four fill shapes and are applied to
    yolov8s unchanged.
  - Every kind other than k1 and res needs a kernel-visible decision: the core reads rows_in,
    cols_in and plane_bytes from the packet header, so plane geometry is data, but a layout
    whose plane stride is no longer the tensor plane changes what those fields mean.

## What would settle it

Change Placement so a tensor's channel blocks interleave at a five-row band (plane stride
5 x pitch), rebuild yolov8n's container, and run tools/engine_dispatch_floor_split.py on it.
That is a compiler-side change with no xclbin rebuild and no kernel change, and one sitting
would convert every number above into a measurement.
